  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe cold front has pushed through much of the area and is\ncurrently draped across the Wyoming Valley to the Catskills. \nThis can be seen with temperatures of 80 degrees in Scranton \nwhile it is 61 degrees in Binghamton. Precipitation has been \nanafrontal and has become lighter as the afternoon has \nprogressed. Showers will continue to push through NEPA and the \nCatskills into the evening hours, but accumulations will be \nlight. With cold air moving in behind the front, some very light\nlake effect rain showers have developed across areas along and \neast of I-81 north of the Southern Tier. These showers and \nsprinkles will linger into the evening hours before high \npressure building in from the west cuts them off. The surface \nhigh and associated mid-level ridge will move in from the Great \nLakes tonight, keeping winds NWerly and reinforcing CAA. Temps \nwill be 20-25 degrees cooler tonight vs. last night, with temps \nbottoming out in the low to mid 40s for most of the region, with\nupper 30s possible across N Oneida county. \n\nHigh pressure will remain overhead Saturday into Sunday morning,\nwith dry conditions and cooler than normal temperatures.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nThe ridge pushes east on Sunday as a broad troughing pattern\nworks its way into the eastern US on Sunday into Sunday night.\nThis pattern will have 2 pieces of energy; one over the northern\nPlains and Great Lakes and another one moving up the eastern\nseaboard. These two shortwaves in the overall longwave trough\npattern are currently forecast to remain out of phase, which\nwill limit precipitation development over the region and bring a\nlow end chance for rain showers across eastern portions of the\narea Sunday afternoon and evening. These shortwaves are\nforecast to phase well east of us Sunday night into Monday,\ngenerating a surface low off the New England coast that will\npush into Canada. Guidance has been pretty consistent with the\ntiming and track of the aforementioned shortwaves so confidence\nis becoming higher that we will not see widespread rain develop\non Sunday. \n\nA cold front is expected to move into the area Monday. Temps at\n850mb near 0C combined with very warm Lake America water temps \nwill kick off lake effect clouds across much of the area along \nwith a chance for some isolated rain showers. Temperatures to \nstart the week will be quite chilly for this time of year as the\nposition of the ridge will reinforce CAA and push the coldest \nair of the season into the region. Average temps for the \nbeginning of October range in the mid to upper 60s in our area. \nOn Monday, we are expected to top out in the upper 50s for most,\nwith low 60s in the valleys across the Southern Tier into NEPA \nand the Catskills. Tuesday will be even cooler, with highs in \nthe low to mid 50s for most. Lows will be quite cold Monday \nnight and Tuesday night, with lows in the low to mid 30s both \nnights(Tuesday will be a few degrees cooler). With some areas \nexpected to see freezing temperatures, especially Tuesday night,\ntake appropriate measures with what remains of your garden or \nany sensitive plants that may be outside. \n\nBy Wednesday the ridge axis will slide east of the area, \nbringing a return of SW flow. This will start a warming trend\nthat will push temperatures back toward average by the end of\nthe week. Conditions are forecast to remain dry through this\nperiod as well.",
